# =============================================================================
#  TypR tutor  ·  Lesson 12 of 12  ·  Your own program
# =============================================================================
#
#  You now know the heart of TypR:
#
#    let x: T <- ...;           values and the four primitive types
#    fn(a: T): U { ... }        typed functions
#    x |> f(y)   x.f(y)         three ways to call any function
#    type R <- list { ... }     records, built with R:{ ... }
#    .Tag(T) | .Other           unions, opened with an exhaustive 'match'
#    Option<T>                  generics
#    @f: (T) -> U;              typing R functions
#
#  and that the compiler is a colleague who reads your code before it runs.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  >>> EXERCISE   (free style)
#
#  Erase this lesson and write a small program of your own, on any subject
#  you like (a to-do list, a shop, a game...). It must compile, and use at
#  least:  a 'type', a typed function 'fn(', and a 'match'.
#
#  Stuck for an idea? Model a traffic light: .Red | .Orange | .Green, and a
#  function that returns the next colour.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

